# /etc/hunter/ports — the listening ports hunter's network monitor expects.
#
# A port that starts listening and is not here is a WARN event in category
# NET, naming the program (see `hunterctl net`). The monitor only watches: it
# never blocks a port. One directive per line:
#
#   expect <tcp|udp|any> <port|lo-hi> [<program>]
#
# Naming the program makes the rule hold only for it: `expect tcp 22
# /usr/sbin/sshd` does not make port 22 fine for a shell that took it first.
# Listeners on 127.0.0.1 or ::1 only are exempt (`ignore-loopback on`).
#
# `hunterctl expect-port` and lunarhunter's "Permitir" button add lines here;
# `hunterctl forget-port` and "Quitar" take them out. The DHCP clients (udp 68
# and 546) are expected without a line.
#
# expect tcp 22 /usr/sbin/sshd
# expect tcp 80 /usr/sbin/nginx
# expect any 6000-6010
